  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Cold, wet conditions through Monday morning and a winter storm\n   for the front range and southwest Montana.\n\n-  Hard freeze potential early next week.\n\n-  Warming up to above normal late next week. \n\n\nOverview & Winter Impacts: An upper-level trough settling over \nthe Pacific Northwest will advect moisture across the Northern \nRockies. Concurrently, a cold front is gradually spilling over the\nContinental Divide, driving gusty east-to-northeast winds and \npushing much colder air south and west. This synoptic setup will \nyield a highly active weekend, featuring accumulating snow \nprimarily focused above 4,000 feet. Expect notable travel impacts \nover regional mountain passes, including Rogers, MacDonald, \nHomestake, and Lost Trail. Total snowfall accumulations through \nMonday morning are forecast to range from 6 to 10 inches across \nthe backcountry of southwest and west-central Montana, while lower\nvalley locations will generally see less than an inch.\n\nHowever, forecast models continue to show spread regarding the \ninland extent of remnant moisture from Tropical Storm Rachel \nSunday night. Should this moisture track further west than current\nconsensus dictates, grassy surfaces in the lower valleys of west-\ncentral and southwest Montana could see locally higher snowfall \ntotals by Monday morning.\n\nRoad & Backcountry Hazards: Recent anomalous warmth has resulted \nin elevated ground temperatures, complicating snow accumulation. \nPrecipitation will initially melt on paved surfaces; however, as \ndiurnal cooling takes over after sunset or where intense snowfall \nrates overcome ground heat, slushy conditions will quickly \ntransition to snow-covered roads, particularly over the passes. \nThis combination of wet snow, falling temperatures, and gusty \nwinds will create an elevated risk for hypothermia for backcountry\nrecreationists. Those heading outdoors should be fully prepared \nfor rapidly deteriorating, raw, and winter-like conditions.\n\nLocalized Wind Threat: A secondary, yet significant, concern is \nthe potential for localized strong east wind gusts exceeding 50 \nmph, focused along the east and southeast shores of Flathead Lake \nand the Lake Blaine area. These crosswinds could lead to minor \ntree damage and localized power outages, with Highway 35 being \nparticularly vulnerable. Recent high-resolution model guidance \nindicates both an earlier onset (mid-afternoon today) and a \nprolonged duration (lasting through at least Sunday afternoon). \nConsequently, the existing Wind Advisory has been updated to \nreflect this expanded timeframe.\n\nEarly Week Freeze: Looking ahead to early next week (Monday \nthrough Wednesday mornings), there is high confidence in a \nwidespread hard freeze across the western Montana valleys, along \nwith near-freezing temperatures for the lower elevations of north-\ncentral Idaho. The primary forecast wild card for Monday morning \nis the extent of lingering cloud cover, which could inhibit \nradiational cooling and keep temperatures slightly warmer than \ncurrent probabilistic guidance suggests.\n\nIn the extended forecast, ensembles remain in strong agreement \nthat upper-level ridging will quickly rebound across the region. \nBy late next week, expect afternoon high temperatures to recover \ninto the 60s across western Montana and the 70s for the lower \nelevations of central Idaho.",
